 Look, I know this isn't easy. Nobody likes learning online, and I think you guys all deserve to be congratulated for

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
 kind of getting through this. It's a really tough time. You're doing a great job. I'm really proud of you.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 As I promised, it's the end of the week, and we're going to take a step back and look at the big picture again.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
 In this video, I want to talk about what are called the seven fundamental components.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,000
 This week, we met some components that had analogues to the ones we met last week. I hope that you noticed this.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
 Rotating masses behave very similarly to the translating masses that we met last week.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
 They had very similar CCRs, the same for torsion and linear springs and friction in both kind of domains.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:52,000
 And I know this is a little bit contrived. You would imagine that springs irrespective of whether they're torsion springs or linear springs would behave the same.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
 Actually, the reason they behave the same is not because they're both springs. It's because they're both capacitors.

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 And I know this sounds crazy, right? We haven't even touched electrical systems yet.

12
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 And here I am telling you that springs are capacitors, but actually underneath the hood, when you look at things purely in terms of energy,

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 in reality, there are only seven kind of fundamental components.

14
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
 In this video, I want to talk about what those things are, how we can recognize them, and why springs and capacitors are the same thing.

15
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 To get started, let's talk about what are called generalized inertias.

16
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 These are one port components, one power port. Do you remember we talked about power ports last week?

17
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 If not, I'll put a link to that video in the description.

18
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
 These are one port components that relate momentum to flow.

19
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 And when I say momentum, I don't mean linear momentum or angular momentum, the specific kinds that we met this week.

20
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,000
 I mean generalized momentum. A quantity that's going to permeate through all the domains that we're looking at.

21
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
 And they do this through a kind of general and potentially nonlinear function that we're going to call Phi.

22
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 The only other important thing for me to note at this stage, I think, is that generalized inertias, they store energy in a kind of kinetic form.

23
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
 You remember last week that I was talking about how you can decompose energy in the system to a kinetic part in a potential part?

24
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 These are the elements that store the kinetic part.

25
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 And they store it in that they can take it and release it later on in contrast to another kind of element will meet generalized resistors.

26
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:30,000
 These are elements that also have one power port and a CCR that relates effort to flow through a relation like this.

27
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
 Again, we're using the notation Phi to mean some potentially nonlinear general function.

28
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
 And for the mechanical system, we've already met these generalized resistors took the form of friction or dampers.

29
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 We related velocity to resistive force irrespective of the domain generalized resistors work to remove energy from our systems.

30
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 They take what would otherwise be useful energy and they convert into heat.

31
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
 Generalized capacitors are also one port components.

32
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
 And these are components that relate generalized displacement, the derivative of flow to effort.

33
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:07,000
 For mechanical systems, we already met generalized capacitors in the form of springs, right?

34
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:12,000
 For the translated mechanical systems, they related displacement, linear displacement to force.

35
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 And for rotating mechanical systems, they related angular displacement to torque.

36
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
 Generalized capacitors just like inertia store energy that we can recover later.

37
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:28,000
 And again, in the context of the discussions we've already had, if you break the energy in a system up to a kind of kinetic part at a potential part,

38
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
 these are the elements that store the potential part.

39
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:40,000
 Generalized ideal effort sources are one port components that provide a certain effort irrespective of the amount of flow that's required to make that happen.

40
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
 We met these already as sources of force or torque.

41
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
 And they're not really a great reflection of reality.

42
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:53,000
 You can imagine that since a generalized effort source will provide whatever flow is required to make that effort happen,

43
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
 it's possible for these elements to provide infinite power.

44
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:09,000
 This is obviously not the case for real life things, but the closest approximation to this would be some kind of source to your system that's so be so powerful that any effect your system can have on it's going to be negligible.

45
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 For example, if you had a huge battery powering a circuit, right?

46
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,000
 Huge battery powering a tiny circuit.

47
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:27,000
 Then any kind of current you draw from that battery, which ostensibly we would consider a voltage source is going to be so minimal that the voltage of that battery is probably not going to drop by any substantial amount.

48
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
 In that case, you could treat the battery as an ideal voltage source.

49
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
 It'll provide you whatever voltage you want in your circuit irrespective of the current that's required to make that happen.

50
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:45,000
 Generalized ideal flow sources are also one port components, but they provide a certain flow irrespective of what effort is required to make this happen.

51
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,000
 Generalized ideal transformers are the first two port element that we should talk about.

52
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:57,000
 And these are things that relate effort import one to the effort import two and the flow import one to the flow import two.

53
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
 We met one of these this week, right?

54
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
 The ideal gearbox.

55
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
 And ideal transformers like the ideal gearbox are nominally lossless.

56
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:11,000
 So this means that all the power you put into port one, you should get back out of port two.

57
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:15,000
 All the gearbox does is change the ratio of effort to flow between the ports.

58
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
 You might get port one with lots of effort and not much flow.

59
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:25,000
 And port two with lots of flow, but not much effort, but the product of these things at both ports should always be the same.

60
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:35,000
 transformers have this generalized CCR where M is a function we call the modulus of the transformer and M inverse is kind of the inverse function.

61
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:42,000
 You notice that the relationship from port one to port two for effort is the inverse of the relationship from port one to port two of flow.

62
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
 This is the way the transformers work.

63
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:47,000
 This is the way that they maintain the same power.

64
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:53,000
 Finally, generalized gyrators, our seventh component are also to port elements.

65
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:01,000
 And they're kind of like transformers except they relate the effort import one to the flow import two and the flow import one to the effort import two.

66
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
 This is kind of the cross pollination I was talking about in the gearbox video.

67
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
 We haven't met any kind of mechanical gyrators yet.

68
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
 They're probably a little bit harder to talk about in the mechanical domain.

69
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 But we're going to meet our first one next week when we talk about electrical circuits.

70
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:23,000
 The same kind of rules as kind of ideal transformers apply in that all the power you put into port one you need to get out of port two again.

71
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:33,000
 These gyrators again have a generalized CCR it looks like this and of course MFT in this case is again the modulus of the gyrator.

72
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
 Now that we have the terminology and we understand the big picture a little more.

73
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:44,000
 Next week when we meet electrical systems I'm not going to introduce you to resistors and capacitors and inductors.

74
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:56,000
 Again the introduce you to the generalized resistors and the generalized inertia and the generalized capacitor for electrical systems that just happen to be actual resistors and actual capacitors and inductors.

75
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:04,000
 I know this one was a little bit longer and I understand that big picture things aren't always super interesting so thank you for watching see you next week.
